{-
   What a `@caller` parameter may not be - Design-Test.md §11.1's F2.

   The fill is a constant the compiler builds at the call, so every rule here is a consequence of one
   fact: the compiler has to be able to build it, and there is exactly one thing it knows about a call
   site. That vocabulary - `file` and `function` as text, `line` and `column` as numbers - is matched
   by field *name* rather than against a particular declaration, because the record it fills lives in
   a library and not in `Core`. A declaration takes the subset it wants and is told about anything
   else.

   Each of these declines the marker rather than half-setting it. A `@caller` position that cannot be
   filled must not become a position every call site may leave out and nothing fills.
-}

data Site {file: String, line: Int, column: Int, function: String}
data Whence {file: String, whence: Int}
data Widths {file: String, line: String}
data Either = Left(Int) | Right(Int)

-- Not a record at all: there is no single value the location is.
fn scalar(@caller at: Int) -> Int = at

-- A sum has no one set of fields for the fill to write.
fn sum(@caller at: Either) -> Int = 0

-- A field outside the vocabulary, and one inside it at the wrong type.
fn unknownField(@caller at: Whence) -> Int = at.whence
fn wrongWidth(@caller at: Widths) -> Int = 0

-- The text fill names a parameter of this function, and produces a `String`.
fn noSuchSource(@caller(source: absent) text: String) -> Int = 0
fn notText(x: Int, @caller(source: x) text: Int) -> Int = text
fn itself(@caller(source: text) text: String) -> Int = 0

-- The fill is a value, so there is no storage of the caller's for a `&` or a `return` to name.
fn mutable(@caller &at: Site) -> Int = at.line
fn rooted(@caller return at: Site) -> Int = at.line

-- `@caller` *is* what fills a position the call site left out, so a written default is a second
-- answer to the same question.
fn alsoDefaulted(x: Int, @caller(source: x) text: String = "") -> Int = 0

-- And a function *type* has no declaration for the fill to be read off.
fn typed(f: (@caller at: Site) -> Int) -> Int = 0

fn main() -> Int = 0
